      "decision": {
        "disposition": "adopt",
        "changes": {
          "control/recipe/holding_tip_speed_weight": 0.01,
          "control/recipe/terminal_tip_speed_weight": 0.01
        },
        "rationale": "Accepted diagnosis (bound baseline-replay inventory, 35 retained updates, single execution): terminal reach is recorded as passed with terminal tip error 0.007642608585867386 m against the 0.01 m reach tolerance [fff8ec664f4de, f12f1083f9849], but terminal tip speed is recorded 0.6351726092425104 m/s [ffad04d70b19c] and the final holding window (6 selected samples, t 0.3-0.35 s [fa54c1ae23d4d, f5cdbebaa75dc]) records tip speed 0.6351726092425104-1.5810152739742807 m/s [f952823a6555c, ff296c4b0c654] against the 0.02 m/s settling speed limit [f4ab3008c7e24], with holding position and sampled settling both recorded as failed [f5ba2ab7e1107, f641da2506e2e]. The retained recipe records terminal_tip_speed_weight = 0.0 [f43a886f15dbe] and holding_tip_speed_weight = 0.0 [f7264b775e0bb], so the objective that produced this record contains no tip-speed cost term at all. Hypothesis: the recorded deviation is concentrated in tip speed, and an absent tip-speed penalty is a plausible contributor to that deviation, so positive values on these two terms should drive the sampled tip-speed band toward the 0.02 m/s settling limit. Relevance of this weight to this observation: these are the only two editable handles acting on squared tip speed, they are currently exactly zero so any positive value is an effective delta, and tip_speed_scale_m_s = 0.02 m/s, scheduling and structure remain unchanged. Expected effects, stated separately: holding speed (primary) - the scheduled holding-node sum should lower window tip speed below its current 0.635-1.581 m/s band; terminal reach - possible small degradation, since a terminal braking term can move the final approach while the terminal position margin under the 0.01 m tolerance is limited; holding position - secondary, plausibly improved if reduced speed reduces overshoot, but could be unchanged; computation - the objective gains terms and may raise iterations/nonconvexity, and nothing in the retained record indicates per-update wall time (already ~17 s against a 0.01 s control period [fb97841323574, ff74842fa741e, fc06e64bbb5d7]) would improve. Value selection is by mechanism only, since raw weight magnitudes are not calibrated against cost contributions or scaling; 0.01 on each weight is a bounded first choice, not a computed optimum, and no raw scalar objective will be compared across weights. Possible adverse effects: eroded reach margin, worse solve cost or deadline behaviour, and, with no causal attribution available, a null result cannot be read as proof that the term is irrelevant. Observations that would weaken the hypothesis: holding-window tip speed still ~0.6-1.6 m/s after the change; reach error crossing 0.01 m; terminal tip speed still far above 0.02 m/s; or deadline misses and per-update wall times materially worse than the baseline record. All other scientific fields remain fixed. A local 0.01 s projected-model comparison is omitted, not because it is unavailable but because a <=10 ms horizon probe cannot represent the 0.05 s settling window or closed-loop settling, so it would test neither the failing window quantity nor this hypothesis.",
        "expected_measurable_effect": "On the next complete execution of the single changed candidate, compared with baseline-replay: the final holding-window tip speed (scheduled 0.05 s window, same 0.02 m/s speed limit) differs measurably from the recorded 0.6351726092425104-1.5810152739742807 m/s band, reported separately from terminal reach (terminal tip error against the 0.01 m tolerance), holding-window position error (0.01 m limit), and computation (per-update wall time and deadline misses). A reduction, an increase, or no measurable change are each informative for this bounded experiment.",
        "local_comparison": null
      },
